Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["FUNCTIONAL PROGRAMMING STYLE",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Title"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Functional programming style applies functions to expression rather than \
through control of execution flow to achieve the computational result.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_]\  := \ Times\  @@ \ Range[n]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
@@ is the Apply[ ] operator; Times @@ expr is short form for Apply[Times, \
expr]. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Range[4]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1, 2, 3, 4}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Apply[Times, \ {1, 2, 3, 4}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(24\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Apply[Plus, \ {1, 2, 3, 4}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(10\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Observe that:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Times\  @@ \ \((a + b + c)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\ b\ c\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Map[f, expr] is another function of similar style.", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[f, \ h[a, b, c]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[f[a], f[b], f[c]]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[Sin, \ {1, \ Pi/2, \ 2  Pi}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Sin[1], 1, 0}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Concept of Pure Function in Mathematica", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
The first argument in Apply[ ], or Map[ ] requires just the name of the \
function, e.g., Sin, not Sin[x].  We call such objects pure functions.  You \
can think of a pure function as a mathematical operator, ready to act on an \
expression.   Here is a general way of defining a pure function.  Note that \
Function[x,x^2] is equivalent to #^2 &.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Function[x, \ x^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Function[x, x\^2]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[\ #^2\ \  &, \ {1, 2, 3}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1, 4, 9}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let me show you a program that find zero of a function, ",
  StyleBox["f(x)=0",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", by applying FixedPoint[ ]; another example of functional programming \
style.  First the built-in function:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?FixedPoint\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("FixedPoint[f, expr] starts with expr, then applies f repeatedly until \
the result no longer changes."\)], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[f_, \ x0_]\  := \ 
      Module[\ {x, \ fp = f'}, \ \[IndentingNewLine]x\  = \ 
          FixedPoint[\((# - f[#]/fp[#])\) &, \ 
            x0]; \[IndentingNewLine]x\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[\((Cos[#] - #)\) &, \ 0.0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0.7390851332151607`\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FindRoot[\ Cos[x] \[Equal] x, \ {x, \ 0}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({x \[Rule] 0.739085133385284`}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
We can give newtonzero another definition so that it accept normal function \
(not pure function) as input.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[expr_, \ x_Symbol, \ x0_Real]\  := 
      newtonzero[Function[x, expr], \ x0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[Cos[x]\  - \ x, \ x, \ 0.3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0.7390851332151607`\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Here is the way usage message is entered for newtonzero, you get the message \
when enter ?newtonzero:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero::usage\  = "\<newtonzero[f,x0] finds f[x]=0, with trial \
starting value x0.  f must be a pure function while x0 should be Real.  \
newtonzero[expr, x, x0] finds x such that expr=0, where expr is an expression \
in the variable x (a symbol), and x0 is the starting value (a Real). N.B. the \
iterations need not always converge.\>"\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "More single-line ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " definitions:\nVector calculus"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Grad[f_, \ arg_]\  := \ \(D[f, #] &\)\  /@ \ arg\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Div[v_, \ arg_]\  := \ Inner[D, \ v, \ arg, \ Plus]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(JocobianMatrix[f_, \ v_]\  := \ Outer[D, \ f, \ v]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TripleProduct[a_, \ b_, \ c_]\  := \ Det[{a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Grad[x^2 + y^2 + z^2, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({2\ x, 2\ y, 2\ z}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]]
},
FrontEndVersion->"4.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 695}},
WindowSize->{1016, 668},
WindowMargins->{{0, Automatic}, {Automatic, 0}},
Magnification->3,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]

